prompt:
I need a wall shelf modelled and then drawn, because the shop that is cutting it works from drawings, not from files.

Call the part `shelf`. It is three panels of the 18 mm ply in stock - the thickness, the shelf's envelope and the cable bore are all in `globals.py`, so read them through `hc` rather than repeating the numbers. Everything in millimetres:

- the deck is 600 wide (along X) and 250 deep (out from the wall), one panel thick, centred left-to-right on x = 0, with its underside on z = 0 and its back edge against the wall face at y = 0 (so it spans y = 0 to 250 and z = 0 to 18);
- it carries one cable pass-through: an 8 mm round hole straight down through the deck, centred on the deck (x = 0, y = 125);
- under each end hangs a side panel, one panel thick, the full 250 mm deep, 200 mm tall, hanging below the deck (z = -200 to 0). Their outer faces are flush with the ends of the deck, so the assembly is exactly 600 mm wide overall;
- the sides must sit against the wall face too. The project already contains a `rail` part - that is the wall itself, reference geometry for the acceptance checks. Leave it exactly as it is.

The drawing is part of the deliverable, not an afterthought, so the part has to carry its own manufacturing metadata: describe it, state the material as `18 mm Baltic birch plywood`, the process as `laser_cut`, the stock form, a general tolerance and the finish. Then produce the dimensioned drawing sheet for `shelf` on A4 - the shop reads the overall sizes, the material thickness and the bore diameter off the sheet, so they have to be on it.

Build the part before you draw it.
